{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And the boys grew.</b> Rashi explained: as long as they were young, their deeds were not recognizable. Once they became thirteen years old, this one parted to the house of study and this one parted to idolatry — a parable to a thorn-bush and a myrtle that grow together; as long as they are small, a person does not distinguish between them; once they have grown, this one has its fragrance and this one its thorns. [Bereshit Rabbah]. And if you ask: according to Rashi's explanation, Esau was only thirteen years old when he parted to idolatry — but he himself explained ״ויזד יעקב נזיד״ that that day Abraham died, so that Abraham would not see his grandson go out to evil culture, and this is the 'good old age' that the Holy One, blessed be He, promised him. And according to what we have said, that he was thirteen years old — if so, he rebelled during Abraham's lifetime for two years, for Abraham lived 175 years, and he was a hundred years old when Isaac was born, and Isaac was sixty years old when Esau was born, and from there to 175 there are fifteen years, so Esau was fifteen years old when Abraham died; if so, he rebelled two years during his lifetime. In the Yerushalmi they raise this difficulty thus, and answer: for two years he rebelled in secret, i.e., stealthily, but after his death he rebelled publicly."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:03.115392275Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}